{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfxqnoepdyCR3wwDKkbMK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I finished all four steps, using only the Kibana tools.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' reads index `kibana_sample_data_logs`, and its time field is `timestamp`. It also has `@timestamp` and `utc_time` date fields, but `timestamp` is the one set as the time field. The HTTP response code is stored as text, in `response` and `response.keyword`.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created 'S4-92dead82 Web logs' with exactly two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart counting requests per time interval.\n   - \"Requests by HTTP response code\": a bar chart counting requests for each `response.keyword` value, top 20.\n3. **Alert rule:** I created 'S4-92dead82 5xx errors' as an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive within 5 minutes. Because the response code is text, a number range wouldn't work, so the rule matches `response.keyword` against the pattern `5[0-9][0-9]`. That covers every code from 500 to 599. I created the rule enabled, so it didn't need a separate enable step.\n4. **Panel edit and checks:** I renamed panel 1 to 'Responses by code', and reading the dashboard back shows the new title. Reading the rule back shows it enabled, with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":233,"cache_read_input_tokens":21145,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":233},"output_tokens":499,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0da1e72d-e9d9-58d9-b388-fca8d0eba393","uuid":"ef87a7ea-94ed-4da9-9894-3c6ed2504cbb"