le Data Logs' reads index `kibana_sample_data_logs`, and its time field is `timestamp`. It also has `@timestamp` and `utc_time` date fields, but `timestamp` is the one set as the time field. The HTTP response code is stored as text, in `response` and `response.keyword`.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created 'S4-92dead82 Web logs' with exactly two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart counting requests per time interval.\n   - \"Requests by HTTP response code\": a bar chart counting requests for each `response.keyword` value, top 20.\n3. **Alert rule:** I created 'S4-92dead82 5xx errors' as an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive within 5 minutes. Because the response code is text, a number range wouldn't work, so the rule matches `response.keyword` against the pattern `5[0-9][0-9]`. That covers every code from 500 to 599. I created the rule enabled, so it didn't need a separate enable step.\n4. **Panel edit and checks:** I renamed panel 1 to 'Responses by code', and reading the dashboard back shows the new title. Reading the rule back shows it enabled, with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes","ttft_ms":1960,"type":"result","duration_ms":27551,"uuid":"7a89355d-1e78-438b-927a-efd8930f2ef5","ttft_stream_ms":1716,"time_to_request_ms":72,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":19,"system_prompt":8,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":10,"input_hooks":4,"tool_pool_refresh":2,"init_message":2,"engine_pickup":2,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":2,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":0,"tool_schema_build":10,"message_normalization":1,"client_creation":2,"request_build":5},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":22,"time_to_request_cpu_ms":93,"time_to_request_major_faults":0,"input_attachments_detail":{"slowest_producer":"queued_commands","slowest_producer_ms":5,"cpu_ms":13,"major_fau